course: dictionary
lesson: 7641
title: Profit: what is left over
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Profit is the money you have left after you take away what you spent from what you earned. In maths, profit = selling price − cost price. A cake costs 50p to make and sells for £1, so the profit is £1 − 50p = 50p.
explain: «Margin is the profit on each item: a bracelet sold for £1 that cost 20p makes 80p, so 10 sold make 10 × 80p = £8.» First work out the profit on one bracelet, then multiply by 10 to find the profit on all of them.
explain: Money coming in is not profit on its own. «Many people mix up takings and profit; profit is only what is left after costs.» Takings are everything you sell. Profit is what is left when you take the costs away. If the costs are bigger than the money coming in, there is a loss instead.
fact: Profit is the money left after costs are taken away.
fact: Profit = selling price − cost price.
fact: If costs are bigger than the money you take in, you make a loss instead of a profit.
fact: As a verb, «to profit» means to make money from something, or to gain something good from it.
fact: At a school fair, pupils work out the profit from a cake stall by taking the costs away from the money raised.
exercise: A shop buys a toy for £4 and sells it for £7. What is the profit? | £11 | £2 | *£3
exercise: A bracelet sells for £1 and cost 20p to make. What is the profit on one bracelet? | 20p | *80p | £1.20 | £8
exercise: A stall takes £10 and its costs are £15. What is the result? | *A loss of £5 | No money at all | A profit of £5 | A profit of £25
test: Takings are £40 and costs are £25. What is the profit? | *£15 | £65 | £10
test: Ankur says takings and profit are the same thing. Who is right? | Ankur, because profit means money coming in | Ankur, takings are always profit | It depends on the day | *Nobody, takings are everything sold, but profit is what is left after costs
exam: Ella buys 10 bags for 30p each and sells them all for 60p each. What is her total profit? | £6 | *£3 | £9 | £1.50
